== Proposal for changing synonyms and antonyms ==

I really hate to suggest such changes when our manpower is so limited and when we have so many words to add, but I think that the way we show synonyms and antonyms now is a little confusing. Currently, they come at the end of the lemma entry. In other words, if a word is both a noun and a verb, there are synonyms and antonyms listed after the noun entries and again after the verb entries. Because of this position, it's often difficult to see which sense the synonym is related to. It's possible to gloss each sense, but that's rarely done and often still not clear. I think the better way to do this is to list the synonyms after each definition, perhaps in purple or another colour the way the Longman Dictionary of Contemporary English does it.
It would look something like this:

{| border=0 width=100% class="{{creation helper|noun|charge|~|~}}"
|-
|bgcolor="#e2e2ff" valign=top width=49%|
[[singular|Singular]]<br>
'''[[charge]]'''
| width=0.5% |
|bgcolor="#e2e2ff" valign=top width=49%|
[[plural|Plural]]<br>
{{#ifeq:|none|''none''|{{creatable|plural|charge|'''[[charges]]'''}}}}{{#if:|&nbsp;or&nbsp;{{creatable|plural|charge|'''[[{{{3}}}]]'''}}|}}
| width=0.5% |
|}
# {{context|label=countable|set=|call=}} A '''charge''' is a claim the somebody has done something wrong or illegal, especially a claim by police.
#: ''He says '''charges''' against his son are false.''
#: ''The police have filed criminal '''charges''' against the company.''
#: ''They will ask the judge to drop the murder '''charge''' against Sutherland.''
#: ''Jowdy denied the '''charges''' of lying.''
# {{context|label=countable|set=|call=}} A '''charge''' for something is the [[price]] of the thing.  It often it is for a [[service]] or [[fee]] for a [[period]] of time. '''<u>( = [[price]], [[cost]], [[rate]], [[fee]], [[fare]])</u>'''
#: ''The '''charge''' for having an item [[ship]]ped [[vary|varies]] [[according]] to the [[weigh]]t''.
#: ''The  '''charge''' each [[month]] for the [[phone]] service is [[fifty]] dollars''.
#: ''The university agreed to let them use the space free of '''charge'''. ''
#: ''Credit card companies must list the fees and interest '''charges''' separately on your monthly bill. ''
#: ''Do you want to put that on your '''charge''' card? ''
# {{context|label=countable|set=|call=}} If someone is in '''charge''' of something, they are responsible for it. '''<u>( =  [[care]], [[control]], [[responsibility]])</u>'''
#: ''Josh is in '''charge''' of buying drinks for the party.''
#: ''He took good care of the children in his '''charge'''.''
# {{context|label=countable|set=|call=}} If someone leads the '''charge''', they are working hard to make some kind of change. '''<u>( =  [[fight]], [[attack]]; ≠ [[retreat]])</u>'''
#: ''Amazon.com led the '''charge''' in making electronic books popular.''

What do you think?--[[User:Brett|Brett]] ([[User talk:Brett|talk]]) 14:27, 16 February 2011 (UTC)
