=== Pronunciation === * {{enPR|grāt}}, {{IPA|/ɡreɪt/}} * {{audio|en-us-grate.ogg|US}} * {{hmp|great}}
== Verb == {{verb|grat|e}} [[File:Grate-cheese.jpg|thumb|Grate-cheese]] # {{transitive}} When you '''grate''' something, you turn it into little pieces by rubbing it against something [[sharp]] or [[rough]]. #: ''He '''grated''' an entire block of [[cheese]] for the [[nachos]].'' # {{transitive}} When you '''grate''' something, you rub it against something in a way that makes a [[loud]], [[annoying]] [[noise]]. #: ''During the [[math]] test, the [[student]] '''grated''' his [[pencil]] against the side of his [[desk]] so loudly that the [[teacher]] [[threaten]]ed to throw the pencil out the [[window]].'' # {{transitive}} When a person '''grates''', they say something in a loud, often annoying voice. #: ''"Silence during tests!" the teacher '''grated''' in a voice that was almost as loud as the pencil.'' # {{intransitive}} When someone or someone '''grates''' on you, it makes you annoyed. It is hard to deal with or listen to. #: ''She means well, but she has a loud, [[high]], [[perky]] voice that just '''grates''' on me.'' #: ''The [[constant]] sound of [[construction]] outside my house '''grated''' on my [[nerves]] until I had to leave.''
== Noun == {{noun}} # {{countable}} A '''grate''' is a [[metal]] [[cover]] with [[bar]]s on it. It may cover things like [[fireplace]]s or [[sewer]]s. #: ''The construction workers covered the [[hole]] in the [[road]] with a ''grate'' so nobody would fall down the hole.''

=== Pronunciation === * {{enPR|hēp}}, {{IPA|/hiːp/}} * {{audio|en-us-heap.ogg|Audio (US)}}
== Noun == {{noun}} # {{countable}} A '''heap''' is a big [[pile]] of things, usually thrown in the pile and not in any [[order]]. #: ''When we moved into our new house, the old owners had left a '''heap''' of [[garbage]] inside for us to deal with.'' #: ''I dumped all of my things into a '''heap''' on my bed so I could start {{r|organiz|ing}} them.'' # {{idiom}} A '''heap''' is a lot of something. #: ''If that dog eats my [[shoe]]s again, he will be in a '''heap''' of trouble.'' #: ''She earned '''heaps''' of money when she was 20 and now she doesn't have to work for the rest of her life.''
== Verb == {{verb}} # {{transitive}} If you '''heap''' something, you pile it up high. #: ''{{lc|Pudding}} is my [[favorite]] [[dessert]]. I '''heaped''' it so high on my [[plate]] that it looked like a little [[mountain]].'' # {{transitive}} If you '''heap''' something, you give a lot of it. #: ''The [[police]] '''heaped''' the children with [[praise]] after they would not get in a car with someone they did not know.'' #: ''Her [[boss]] [[surprise]]d her by '''heaping''' the [[blame]] for the [[project]] onto her, when really, the problems were his own [[fault]].''

===Pronunciation===
* {{UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɛk.spəˌdaɪt/}}
* {{US}} {{IPA|/ˈɛk.spɪˌdaɪt/}}
* {{audio|en-us-expedite.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en-au-expedite.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{hyph|ex|pe|dite}}

== Verb ==
{{verb|expedit|e}}
# {{transitive}} When you '''expedite''' something, you make it happen faster.
#: ''We ran out of [[supplies]] at the free [[health]] [[clinic]] very quickly.  The Director promised to '''expedite''' the [[process]] of getting us more supplies, but a week later we still had none.''
#: ''After Latanya spent 16 hours trying to [[give birth]], the [[doctor]] tried '''expediting''' her labor by giving her medicines. After that, she gave birth in less than an hour.''
#: ''The Post Office [[offer]]s "'''expedited''' [[delivery]]" for [[package]]s that need to get sent very quickly.''

=== Pronunciation ===
* {{US}} {{IPA|/tʃɑr/|[tʃɑr]|[tʃɑ˞]}}
* {{UK}} {{IPA|/tʃɑː/}}
* {{audio|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-char.wav &lrm;|UK}}

== Verb ==
{{verb|char|r}}
# {{transitive}} When you '''char''' something, you [[burn]] it, usually until it is black.
#: ''Joseph forgot that the [[steak]] was cooking.  By the time he remembered, it was '''charred''' so badly it looked like a black [[brick]] and tasted like [[ash]]es.''
#: ''The house fire was so hot that the walls were '''charred''.''

== Noun ==
{{noun|char|char|chars}}
# '''Char''' is something that has been charred.
#: ''After the fire, all the '''char''' on the walls was so thick you could [[scrape]] it off.''
#: ''After I use a [[barbecue]] [[grill]], it always takes me forever to scrape the '''char''' off the grill.''
# {{sense|UK}} A '''char''' is a [[clean]]ing woman.
#: ''The family was so [[poor]] that she had her first job as a '''char''' when she was 13.''

=== Pronunciation === * {{IPA|/ɛkspɛkˈteɪʃən/}} * {{audio|en-us-expectation.ogg|US}}
== Noun == {{noun}} {{AVL}} # {{countable}} An '''expectation''' is something you think is going to [[happen]] (something you [[expect]] to happen). #: ''Zeke's [[mother]] had every '''expectation''' that he would go to [[university]] after [[high school]]. So she was surprised when he told her he was joining the [[military]] instead.'' #: ''The doctors told me, "Our '''expectations''' are that these tests will not show any serious problems. But we want to do them anyway just to be safe."'' # {{countable}} An '''expectation''' is something that a person is supposed to do, has to do, or needs to do. #: ''After Latasha forgot to pay her Internet [[bill]], the Internet [[company]] told her: "Our '''expectation''' is that you will pay on time in the future."'' #: ''His [[parent]]s' '''expectations''' were that Kyle would clean his room, make his bed, and make [[dinner]] every night.'' # {{uncountable}} If you do something in '''expectation''', you do it while waiting for something to happen (you are expecting something). #: ''Before they were [[married]], Bill would wait by the [[telephone]] for hours every night in '''expectation''' of Monica's next call.'' #: ''As soon as she was served the [[chocolate]]-covered {{r|strawberr|ies}}, her [[mouth]] began to water in '''expectation''' of how good they would [[taste]].''
=== Related words === * [[expectant]]

=== Pronunciation ===
* {{UK}} {{IPA|/nəʊˈmɛn.klətʃə/}}
* {{US}} {{IPA|/ˈnoʊmənˌkleɪtʃɚ/|/ˌnoʊmənˈkleɪtʃɚ/}}
* {{audio|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-nomenclature.wav|US}}

== Noun ==
{{noun}}
# '''Nomenclature''' is a system for naming things and putting things into categories.  It is used in [[science]], especially [[biology]].
#: ''My science [[teacher]] asked me: "How is your'' Felis catus ''today?"  I had no idea she was using [[scientific]] '''nomenclature''' to ask me how my [[cat]] is.''
#: ''The teacher [[explain]]ed that there are thousands of words for "cat" in different [[language]]s.  However, if you said'' "Felis catus" ''to any scientist in the world, they would know you were talking about a cat.''

=== Pronunciation ===
* {{UK}} {{US}} {{IPA|/ˈpɔɪn.jənt/|/ˈpɔɪɲ.ənt/}}
* {{audio|en-us-poignant.ogg|US}}
* {{audio|en-au-poignant.ogg|AU}}
* {{hyph|poign|ant}}; {{hyph|poi|gnant|nocaption=1}}

== Adjective ==
{{adj}}
# If something is '''poignant''', it gives you [[sadness]] and [[regret]].
#: ''Our walk through the graveyard was a '''poignant''' reminder of life's shortness.''
# If something says something '''poignant''', it is sharp, surprising, and [[accurate]].
#: ''His '''poignant''' remark ended the argument.''

=== Word parts === {{ety-suffix|joint|ly}}
== Adverb == {{adv}} # When two or more people do something '''jointly''', they do it together; they [[cooperate]] on it. #: ''The President told his staff that he liked to make decisions '''jointly'''. He liked to get everybody's [[opinion]] and decide as a group what was best, instead of just making decisions on his own.'' #: ''The [[teacher]] told her [[student]]s: "You may do this project '''jointly'''. Please pick one other student who you would like to work with."'' # When two things cause something '''jointly''', both things caused it to happen. #: ''The [[police]] decided that both [[driver]]s caused the [[car]] [[accident]] '''jointly''', because they were both driving too fast and talking on their [[cell phone]]s.'' #: ''{{lc|Slavery}}, different ideas about [[government]], and many other things were all '''jointly''' [[responsible]] for causing the American Civil War.''
[[Category:Adverbs suffixed with -ly]]

{{see|et al.}}
==Adverb== {{adverb}}
# '''et alia''' means "and others." It is almost always written ''[[et al.]]'' #: ''If you are writing a long list of names, you can use '''et alia''' to make the list shorter. You can write a few names, and then write '''et alia''' to stand for the rest of the names.'' #: ''Since the book was written by ten different [[author]]s, I used '''et al.''' in my [[report]] instead of writing all of their names. I wrote: "In their book, Fallon, Daniels, Stefan, '''et al.''' say that [[chocolate]] is good for you."''
===Usage notes=== * Normally, ''et alia'' and ''et al.'' are only used in [[academic]] writing * ''et alia'' is always written in [[lowercase]] [[letter]]s * ''et alia'' is usually written in [[italics]] * ''et alia'' usually stands for at least two names

== Noun ==
{{noun}}
