== Preposition == {{preposition}} # You use '''aplenty''' after a [[noun]] to say that there is a [[large]] [[amount]] or [[number]] of something. {{syn|galore}} #: ''There were mistakes '''aplenty''' in the report.'' #: ''We have chairs '''aplenty''', so bring your friends.'' # If things are '''aplenty''', there are a lot of them. #: ''In summer, fresh vegetables are '''aplenty''' at the market.'' # If something happens '''aplenty''', it happens a lot. #: ''The team scored '''aplenty''' in the second half.''
=== Usage notes === * '''Aplenty''' doesn't come before a noun, so ''aplenty mistakes'' is wrong. * After a noun, it's almost never used with ''the'' or ''my''. People say ''There were mistakes '''aplenty''''', not ''the mistakes '''aplenty'''''.
